{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792935275,"nonce":"c3e5tQJawmBM6qDpj62cFdz4NLo=","title":"caribou new: scaffold a project","message":"Part of 5ed625d51a511b21c35b0b711cf6ddb898831a2fa7bccabc1ecf3bb1973d123a (the project format RayDE opens).\n\n`caribou new \u003cdir\u003e` writes a project that runs as it is:\n- a `\u003cname\u003e.cbproj` listing Haxe, Lua, Python and Wren;\n- a Haxe entry;\n- one Lua, one Python and one Wren guest module under `src/game/`, each\n  using the others through ordinary imports.\n\nIt is shaped like ChimpMonk's example/guests. RayDE's New Project gallery has\na Caribou starter that runs it. Its requirements are `caribou` and\n`haxe`, and its run command is `caribou run \u003cname\u003e.cbproj`. The CLI and\nthe gallery therefore make the same project.\n\nAn existing directory is never written into, as RayDE's starters never\noverwrite one. A test runs `caribou new` and then `caribou run` on the\nresult.","files":null}]}